# <%= current_page.data.title %>

<%= partial('partials/page_toc') %>

This page covers day to day repository work. It complements the existing
[source code standard](/standards/storing-source-code/#repository-requirements)
and does not replace its repository controls or scanning requirements.

## Ownership and access

- Repository maintainers should ensure at least two named responsible maintainers or an appropriately staffed
  owning team are recorded under the proposed DfE ownership baseline. This is a resilience measure, not a SAML
  requirement.

- Use least privilege and approved teams. Keep README ownership and support details current, and use
  CODEOWNERS to route reviews where configured.

- Request permission before changing repository visibility, access, security settings or deployment behaviour.
  Public content must be suitable for publication under DfE policy.

## Branches pull requests and checks

- Follow the repository CONTRIBUTING guidance. For the Developer Platform repositories, use
  `US/<ticket-number>` branches and the documented `US/<ticket-number>` to `dev` to `main` PR workflow. This
  is a team convention rather than a universal GitHub requirement.

- Make focused changes, link the work item and explain how you tested them. Request an authorised reviewer and
  code owner where required.

- Resolve review conversations and obtain fresh approval when changes invalidate an earlier review. Required
  build, test, format and security checks must pass.

- Do not bypass branch protection, force push a protected branch or merge your own unreviewed changes. Use an
  approved exception process for genuine emergencies.

## Security scanning and alerts

- Repository maintainers should verify secret scanning and push protection, dependency alerts and updates, and
  code scanning or approved equivalent tooling. Availability depends on visibility, licensing and
  configuration; an enterprise account alone does not prove coverage.

- If push protection blocks a secret, stop and investigate. Remove and rotate a real exposed credential as
  appropriate. Do not bypass the protection merely to complete a push.

- Treat Dependabot PRs and AI suggested fixes as changes requiring tests and review. A version update can
  introduce breaking changes.

- Report missing protections to the repository owner or DevSecOps. Alert recipients, triage and remediation
  ownership must be defined; do not assume every contributor receives alerts.

## Using GitHub Copilot

Follow [GitHub Copilot safe use guidance](/guides/github/copilot/).

Review and test every generated change before accepting or merging it. Copilot can produce incorrect or
insecure code. Content exclusions and instructions are not a substitute for access controls or approved
handling of sensitive information. Keep required CI, human review and security checks in place.

## Apply the repository workflow

The `US/<ticket-number>` to `dev` to `main` convention above applies to the
Developer Platform repositories. Follow each repository's own contribution
instructions and protected branches. Do not introduce `dev` or assume that
workflow applies to this technical guidance repository.

Where a required security capability is unavailable, raise the gap with the
repository owner and DevSecOps and agree its resolution through the approved
process. Missing licensing or a deferred implementation does not waive the
existing security requirements.

